<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Nature of stationary points from the gradient</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The curve \(y = {@f@}\) has stationary points at \(x = {@s1@}\) and \(x = {@s2@}\).</p><p>(a) Find the value of \(\dfrac{dy}{dx}\) at \(x = {@t1@}\), at \(x = {@t2@}\) and at \(x = {@t3@}\).</p><div>At \(x = {@t1@}\): [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><div>At \(x = {@t2@}\): [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><div>At \(x = {@t3@}\): [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div><p>(b) The stationary point at \(x = {@s1@}\) is a [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p><p>(c) The stationary point at \(x = {@s2@}\) is a [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>\[\dfrac{dy}{dx} = {@df@} = {@dfac@}\]</p><p>The test values \(x = {@t1@}\), \({@t2@}\) and \({@t3@}\) lie on each side of the stationary points, and \(x = {@t2@}\) lies between them, so it serves as a test value for both.</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(x\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@t1@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@s1@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@t2@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@s2@}\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@t3@}\)</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(\dfrac{dy}{dx}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@g1@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(0\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@g2@}\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(0\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@g3@}\)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Sign</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(g1>0)"]]\(+\)[[else]]\(-\)[[/if]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(0\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(g2>0)"]]\(+\)[[else]]\(-\)[[/if]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">\(0\)</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(g3>0)"]]\(+\)[[else]]\(-\)[[/if]]</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Shape</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(g1>0)"]]rising[[else]]falling[[/if]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">flat</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(g2>0)"]]rising[[else]]falling[[/if]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">flat</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[if test="is(g3>0)"]]rising[[else]]falling[[/if]]</td></tr></table><p>(b) At \(x = {@s1@}\) the gradient goes from [[if test="is(g1>0)"]]\(+\)[[else]]\(-\)[[/if]] to [[if test="is(g2>0)"]]\(+\)[[else]]\(-\)[[/if]]: a [[if test="is(n1=1)"]]maximum point[[elif test="is(n1=2)"]]minimum point[[else]]point of inflection[[/if]].</p><p>(c) At \(x = {@s2@}\) the gradient goes from [[if test="is(g2>0)"]]\(+\)[[else]]\(-\)[[/if]] to [[if test="is(g3>0)"]]\(+\)[[else]]\(-\)[[/if]]: a [[if test="is(n2=1)"]]maximum point[[elif test="is(n2=2)"]]minimum point[[else]]point of inflection[[/if]].</p><p>At a maximum point the curve rises to it and falls after it. At a point of inflection the curve flattens for an instant but carries on the same way.</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
