    <node><name>1</name><description>Wrong A</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans4</sans><tans>wa4</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt4-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[if test="is(n1=1)"]]<p>You chose minimum point. At a minimum point the curve falls to the point and rises after it, so the gradient is negative just before it and positive just after. Compare that with the signs of your gradients in (a) at \(x = {@t1@}\) and \(x = {@t2@}\).</p>[[else]]<p>You chose maximum point. At a maximum point the curve rises to the point and falls after it, so the gradient is positive just before it and negative just after. Compare that with the signs of your gradients in (a) at \(x = {@t1@}\) and \(x = {@t2@}\).</p>[[/if]]]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt4-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[if test="is(n1=3)"]]<p>You chose minimum point. At a minimum point the curve falls to the point and rises after it, so the gradient is negative just before it and positive just after. Compare that with the signs of your gradients in (a) at \(x = {@t1@}\) and \(x = {@t2@}\).</p>[[else]]<p>You chose point of inflection. At a point of inflection the curve flattens for an instant but carries on the same way, so the gradient has the same sign on both sides. Compare that with the signs of your gradients in (a) at \(x = {@t1@}\) and \(x = {@t2@}\).</p>[[/if]]]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
